On 12/21/2012 09:30 PM, Yla Tausczik wrote:
> Hi, I am trying to perform Confirmatory Factor Analysis on a data set
> with a nested design. I want to be able to include the equivalent of
> random intercepts that I would if I were using HLM in order to control
> for error associated with the nested design. I am having trouble
> understanding how to do this in lavaan.
>
> In my data users took part of an experiment in three-person groups.
> After the experiment they filled out a questionnaire. I am performing
> CFA to test the fit of a particular factor structure on the
> questionnaire and I want to do so at the individual level. However, I
> also want to be able to control for the fact that users took part in the
> experiment in groups (which may have introduced random error). My data
> looks something like this:
>
> User | Group | Item 1 | Item 2 | Item 3 ...
> u1 | g1 | 8 | 4 | 4 ...
> u2 | g1 | 4 | 2 | 3 ...
> u3 | g1 | 9 | 2 | 5 ...
> u4 | g2 | 3 | 2 | 5 ...

Have a look at the lavaan.survey package. You can treat 'group' as a
clustering variable, and get a 'robust' solution taking the
cluster-effect into account.

Alternatively, since you only have three subjects within each group,
would it be unreasonable to average the results (of the three subjects)
and do an analysis with groups as units?

Yves.

